From: OpenBMC X570D4I-2T port <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] redfish: StorageController protocols from inventory

Render SupportedControllerProtocols / SupportedDeviceProtocols and
NVMeControllerProperties.ControllerType on
/redfish/v1/Systems/system/Storage/1/Controllers/<id>.

Upstream populateStorageController serves only Id/Name, Status from
Item.Present, and the Asset decorator: there is no D-Bus property it
maps to any protocol or controller-type field, and phosphor's
Inventory.Item.StorageController interface is a bare marker defining no
properties at all. So read an OPTIONAL `Protocol` string property from
that same interface, using the existing Item.Drive DriveProtocol enum
vocabulary (the only storage-protocol enum phosphor defines) and the
existing convertDriveProtocol() mapping from storage_chassis.hpp. A
controller that resolves to NVMe additionally reports
NVMeControllerProperties.ControllerType=IO, and every controller the
inventory can describe attaches over PCIe, so that is the controller
protocol. Controllers without the property just omit the fields, as
before.

On this board the publisher is p2a-inventory-monitor.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/redfish-core/lib/storage_controller.hpp
+++ b/redfish-core/lib/storage_controller.hpp
@@ -5,10 +5,15 @@
 
 #include "app.hpp"
 #include "async_resp.hpp"
+#include "generated/enums/protocol.hpp"
 #include "generated/enums/resource.hpp"
+#include "generated/enums/storage_controller.hpp"
 #include "http_request.hpp"
 #include "query.hpp"
 #include "registries/privilege_registry.hpp"
+#include "storage_chassis.hpp"
+
+#include <optional>
 
 namespace redfish
 {
@@ -47,6 +52,45 @@
 
     asset_utils::getAssetInfo(asyncResp, connectionName, path, ""_json_pointer,
                               false);
+
+    // Optional Protocol property on the StorageController inventory object,
+    // using the Item.Drive DriveProtocol enum vocabulary (there is no
+    // controller-side protocol enum in phosphor-dbus-interfaces). Controllers
+    // that do not publish it simply omit the protocol fields.
+    dbus::utility::getProperty<std::string>(
+        connectionName, path,
+        "xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Item.StorageController", "Protocol",
+        // ast-grep-ignore: long-lambda
+        [asyncResp](const boost::system::error_code& ec,
+                    const std::string& proto) {
+            if (ec)
+            {
+                return;
+            }
+            std::optional<protocol::Protocol> deviceProtocol =
+                convertDriveProtocol(proto);
+            if (!deviceProtocol ||
+                *deviceProtocol == protocol::Protocol::Invalid)
+            {
+                return;
+            }
+            nlohmann::json::array_t deviceProtocols;
+            deviceProtocols.emplace_back(*deviceProtocol);
+            asyncResp->res.jsonValue["SupportedDeviceProtocols"] =
+                std::move(deviceProtocols);
+            // Everything the inventory can describe here is a PCIe-attached
+            // controller (NVMe, or SATA/SAS HBAs hanging off PCIe functions).
+            nlohmann::json::array_t controllerProtocols;
+            controllerProtocols.emplace_back(protocol::Protocol::PCIe);
+            asyncResp->res.jsonValue["SupportedControllerProtocols"] =
+                std::move(controllerProtocols);
+            if (*deviceProtocol == protocol::Protocol::NVMe)
+            {
+                asyncResp->res
+                    .jsonValue["NVMeControllerProperties"]["ControllerType"] =
+                    storage_controller::NVMeControllerType::IO;
+            }
+        });
 }
 
